On 13 Oct 93 23:19:59 GMT sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J.
Whitrow) wrote:

	>In this situation your "freedom" would be illusory. [...] 
	>Since the ability to reason is a prerequisite for "free will"  
	  
All rational thought is deterministic ,the very word "reason"
implies this . Rationality involves connecting ideas coherently,
analytically, logically ,together for a REASON . If thinking is
valid it has a cause , a motive, a reason. Irrational thought
can also be deterministic but it doesn't have to be. Poor
thinking could just be caused by using the wrong algorithm or it
could have no cause and be random and non-deterministic. I don't
think any of these cases will lead you where you want to go.

More than 25 years ago Dr. Jose Delgato of Yale University
approached several people about to undergo brain surgery for
medical reasons and asked if he could implant some electrodes in
their brain at the same time. Some agreed. This experiment might
be difficult to perform today without getting a lot of flack
from self proclaimed ethics experts.

In one case, when the electrode was fired the man would
always turn his head to the left. The interesting thing was that
the man said he felt free, he couldn't even tell when the
electrode was turned on. The patient was always able to come up
with good reasons for turning to the left. He would say "I'm
looking for my slippers" or "I'm restless" or "I heard a noise"
or "I was looking under the bed". This man was intelligent,
rational,( he never said I'm  looking for Martians in my
bathtub) and felt perfectly free yet in all cases he thought
turning his head was his own idea, he wanted to do it.

I don't understand your distinction between "illusion of
freedom" and "real freedom". If you can't tell the difference
objectively, and you can't tell the difference subjectively,
then what's the difference? It cant be that drugs or electricity
were involved because drugs and electricity are always involved,
the brain, after all, is a electro-chemical machine. Much the
same effect can often be obtained in other ways. Someone of
great eloquence and persuasive ability can sometimes make you do
things you would never do otherwise and make you think it was
your own idea. True, you could resist if you wanted to but the
point is, you wouldn't want to.

If you don't like my definition of free will ( a feeling of
choice) try this one "A person has free will if they can
sometimes do what they want to do"; thus the more you can do the
freer you are.   

