>  I gave a copy of the quiz to my favorite 10-year-old, and this was the
>question he asked, still half way thru the quiz (and to the discovery that
>he is an ultra-libertarian), but already impressed by the general direction
>and wisdom of the questions - which led to a very fruitful discussion of
>some ideas on personal and economic freedoms.
>  (The only objection my son had, is that he wanted the to expand the
>concept of sexual consent to include children.  I am sure Perry would agree
>with him, but I personally remain unconvinced.
>  It still doesn't seem right to me to give choice in sex, drugs, etc.
>to, say, 1-year-olds.  But how to draw the lines, and who may do it for
>whom, I find very difficult to decide.
>   Could anybody explain me how the idea of custody would work under PPL?

You would contract into the system you think best for your child, and she
would contract into one she thought best for herself as soon as she could.

I like to think of it in terms of presumption of competence.  I presume every-
one competent to direct themselves until proven otherwise.  Applied to
young people, it seems pretty clear that reasonable people will find that
newborns aren't competent to direct their own lives, but older people will be
found more and more competent as they develop their faculties.  I expect that
for teenagers the decisions of reasonable people would be very mixed, as this
seems to be where most of the existing paradigms break down, but that after
the age of 19 most reasonable people would find people competent to direct
their own lives.

The way I would do this is to give young people the exclusive, non-transferable
or "proxy-able" legal right to challenge any finding that they were
incompetent at a younger age.  Bringing the challenge itself would be an
indicator of some competence, although not necessarily a decisive one.  The
burden of proving their incompetence would be upon their guardian or some
person(s) wanting to be their guardians.

The procedure I sketchily have in mind would be somewhat similar to provisions
we have now for "emancipated minors," which enable young people to get
adult status prior to the standard age.  As I understand them, though, a young
person trying to become an emancipated minor now has to bear the burden of
proving their competence.  I would reverse this.

