Subject:  belief and free will

>From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
>Subject: belief and free will
>
>James joins in:
>> If someone freely chooses to carefully compare his ideas
>> about the world with the evidence, he chooses rightly.  If
>> he refrains from doing this, or if (like Perry or Price) he
>> defines certain kinds of evidence as meaningless and
>> chooses to ignore such evidence, he freely chooses and he
>> chooses wrongly.
>
>Then we can compromise by saying that belief, like debt, can be a
>consequence of voluntary acts, but not itself voluntary.  (Okay, we
>can incur purely voluntary debt, as when we pledge to a charity, but
>my point is that we usually don't.)

A belief is a conscious assertion, an act of consciousness.  Whether
you believe something is subject to volition.  There is nothing you
can do that will "make" you believe X instead of Y unless you choose to,
even if X is warranted by the evidence and Y is not.

For instance, I sometimes choose to believe that the Indians were right
about photos stealing the soul.  There's no reason for this, but I can
believe it anyways.

I think, Anton, you mean more than belief by your description of
disagreement with Christians.  I think you mean that you disagree with
them that you know, e.g., there is a God.  They present you with an
argument you don't find persuasive - i.e., you don't think it's true or
that the truth of the conclusion is warranted by the argument - and you
tell them this.  They then accuse you of ignoring their reasons.

You seem to practice the rule that you will only believe that which you
know - classically defined as justified true belief.  However desirable
this may be, you could choose to do otherwise and believe any old thing.
Many people do.  If they believe something that would guide them into
danger, e.g., "I am bulletproof," then they and their beliefs will be
selected our of the meme pool ("This is the Meme Police!  Out of the
Pool!" to paraphrase Tim May).  If they don't, then they may remain
polluting the meme pool with silly, false, unjustified beliefs that
won't lead to much harm to themselves.

