Date: Sun, 24 Oct 93 3:48:25 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Econometric model quality, AIT challenge

We've seen how AIT helps explain one of the criterion considered
the sign of a good econometric model, parsimony, and how AIT
lets us come up with a formal measure that relates the
parsimony of a model to the probability that it is correct.
Remember, formalization means automation -- which can give us,
for example, a billion times faster discovery of a new arbitrage 
opportunity.  The first guy to automate new formalizations of important 
model criteria beats the other guys to market, and wins the trading 
game.

I'd like to challenge our AIT experts to suggest AIT-derived
models to formalize some of the other qualities of a good
econometric model:

Data coherence -- Is the model consistent with the data?  
The model should provide a good fit to the data.  The residuals, as 
well as being relatively small, should be approximately random.  A 
wide range of tests have been used to investigate departures from 
randomness; is there a general-purpose way to do this?

Consistency with prior knowledge -- Economic theory may provide
prior information on the size or magnitude of various parameters
and the estimated model should be consistent with this information.
More generally, the model parameters and the estimated model should be 
consistent with any prior knowledge, not necessarily that which
derives from economic theory.  (For example, an arbitrage model
might incorporate both economic theory and phenomenon from the hard 
sciences).

Data admissability -- A model should be unable to predict values which
violate definitional cosntraints.  For example, many variables cannot
be negative.

Structural stability -- As well as providing a good fit within
the sample, the exact same model should also give a good fit outside 
the sample.

Encompassing -- A model is said to ecompass a rival formulation
if it can explain the results given by the rival formulation.
If this is the case, the rival model contains no information which
could be used to improve the preferred model.  In order to be
encompassing a model does not need to be more general than its
rivals.  Indeed the notion of parsimonious ecompassing is 
essential to avoid vacuous formulations.  A well-known example
from physics: Einsteinian relativity encompasses Newtonian physics.  
This is non-parsimonious encompassing -- it encompasses Newtonian physics 
by explaining more data, not by explaining the same data with smaller 
models.  An example of parsimonious encompassing would be if Newton at
some point simplified his own model, getting rid of gratuitous variables, 
etc.  In parsimonious encompassing a smaller model explains the
same data.

