Already this sounds promising.  Still, to me the most interesting
question regarding data is stuff beyond price quotes.  Perry has
mentioned that a strategy of picking good companies with good
products in an industry that the picker understands well has
worked for him, and that seems pretty sensible to me as long
as it's done with ruthless honesty and ego-detachment.  From
your various posts, Tim, I gather that something like this is
how you have invested your money and it seems like it's paid
off for you.  The reason I like this approach is that it takes
full advantage of the human biocomputer's ability to correlate
lots of different kinds of information.

As a further example, I notice that whenever J. Random Bloated,
Inc. announces that they're going to lay off a thousand workers,
the stock usually bounces upward.  I guess that the reasons for
this have to do with the "fundamentals" of the slimmed-down
operation and maybe some psychological perception that Bloated's
management is taking an active interest in making the corp.
profitable.  A Truly Impressive Induction Engine would take
such things into account when trying to squeak out a statistical
edge... what tends to happen months down the road after such
actions, for companies of various characteristics?  While
I agree that the price of slippers in Tangiers might not have
any predictable effect on the performance of stocks, it sure
seems like lots of non-price data could.

> I gave up watching CNBC, except for the running ticker, even before I had
