> sophisticated statistical analyis, season with hedging and portfolio
> insurance to adjust the beta to taste.)

Were I to try such a venture, that's _exactly_ what I'd do.  To me,
there are two overwhelmingly difficult problems:  formatting the
relevant data, including prospecti and so on, and reducing it
enough to make the computations tractable.  I remember running
some neural nets on the University's Connection Machine, which
is a substantial amount of processing horsepower, and yet it
still was infuriatingly slow for nets of reasonable size.
Many runs would literally take days with 32 processors chugging
away.  A friend of mine is doing his dissertation in this area --
choosing likely-relevant features from very large candidate
feature pools.  Interesting stuff.

> My friend's work with using a panoply of induction techniques (every
> trendy word, from neural nets to genetic programming to Black-Scholes
> portfolio methods to chaos to expert systems to Bayesian
> statistics....)

Ooh, don't forget fuzzy logic and information-theoretic decision
trees!  Seriously, I think that this approach that your friend is
using is quite promising for gaining a little edge over most
investors.

> Actually, I just saw an ad for a "neural net football predictor"
