I've thought many times about building such a system.  It would
be pretty easy... I'd use a similar approach to what your friend is
using -- a set of induction modules with a neural net above them
to combine their results.  The modules I'd use:
  - backpropagation neural net
  - genetic algorithm (maybe GP but I don't really have the horsepower for it)
  - ID3 or perhaps Fringe
  - Bayes

Data:
  * Of course, things like a complete score result matrix including
	weather and home field, etc, for the past.  This is the easiest
	stuff to come by, but is far less than would be desirable.  Also,
	predictions made by as many pundits as can be found.

  * Each player position would have a vector of statistics, with
    those of the expected starters plugged in when making the
    prediction.  These statistics would include physical characteristics
    of the player, such as speed, weight, and so on, as well as
    performance indicators weighted toward recent performance --
	running backs might have yards/carry, fumbles, yards/pass,
	etc.  These stats are obtainable from the better sports rags
	and are a decent compromise regarding comprehensiveness and
	difficulty of data entry.

There are other subtleties I'd like to play with too.  If I only
had time to do it, it'd be fun to see how well it worked.

> Joe Sixpack loads his "Joe Montana Picks the Winners" CD-ROM into his 3DO
