Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

Derek Zahn:
> ...picking good companies with good
> products in an industry that the picker understands well has
> worked for him,

A challenge is how to seamlessly incorporate this information
into automated models (as new member of a GP function set, rules
incorporated into fitness function, synthetic asset composition
rules, etc.)

Tim May:
> > (Hint: automate a lot of "fundamental" ideas into thousands of if-then
> > rule sets...

If this isn't done carefully, it's a good way to create "brilliant 
pennies" that simply reflect the details of the data in their rules 
rather than incorporating repetitive patterns in the data.  The best 
situation here is if these rules are a good compressions a huge bunch 
of data not in our data set, ie they allow the model to incororporate 
well-documented prior knowledge.  The induction procedure shouldn't
incorporate these rules unless they are necessary for compressing
a pattern from the data at hand.

> > Mix with neural nets

Neural nets are also very prone to creating "brilliant pennies".
Very important to make sure the size of the resulting neural
net is much smaller than the amount of data it explains, thus
indicating it has really learned patterns rather than simply
rote-memorized the data.  Neural nets are also bad for verifying
that a model bears some relation to previous models and otherwise
makes sense.  Much better to do symbolic regression of Diophantine
equations, with primtive function sets including the standard financial
stuff (cash flow, Black-Scholes, Martingales, decision-tree
representations of fixed-income securities, etc.)  Induction
systems that spend their time playing with weird representations
of Turing machines (neural, fuzzy, etc. etc.) instead of composing
Turing-complete representations that can easily incorporate such prior 
knowledge into their models, are going to be relatively weak and difficult
to work with.  In other words, a waste of time.

